6.6R: \\(W\\) is jointly proportional to \\(u\\) and \\(v\\), so \\(W=kuv\\) for one constant \\(k\\). When \\(u=6\\) and \\(v=4\\), \\(W=264\\). Enter the value of \\(k\\). -> ans 11
6.6R: A worker's pay is proportional to the number of shifts worked and to the hours in each shift. Four shifts of 6 hours pay 528 dollars in total. Enter t -> ans 990
6.6R: \\(P\\) is jointly proportional to \\(q\\) and \\(r\\), and \\(P=189\\) when \\(q=3\\) and \\(r=9\\). Enter \\(P\\) when \\(q=5\\) and \\(r=4\\). -> ans 140
6.6R: \\(y\\) is directly proportional to \\(m\\) and inversely proportional to \\(n\\). When \\(m=8\\) and \\(n=6\\), \\(y=24\\). Enter \\(y\\) when \\(m=15\\) and \\(n=10\\ -> ans 27
6.6R: The number of days a water store lasts is proportional to the volume stored and inversely proportional to the number of households drawing on it. 900  -> ans 12
6.6R: A tram covers 78 km in 65 minutes at a steady speed. Enter how many kilometers it covers in 100 minutes at that same speed. -> ans 120
6.6R: A survey drone covers 189 km in 3 hours 30 minutes at a steady speed. Use \\(1\\text{ h}=60\\text{ min}\\). Enter the drone's speed in kilometers per hour -> ans 54
6.6R: A painter finishes a mural in 15 hours, covering the same amount of wall in every hour. Enter the fraction of the mural that is finished once 6 hours  -> ans 2/5
6.6R: One conveyor loads a barge in 14 hours and a second conveyor loads the same barge in 35 hours, each at its own steady rate. Both run at once on one ba -> ans 10
6.6R: Pumps A and B running together drain a tank in 12 hours, and pump A alone drains the tank in 20 hours. Enter how many hours pump B alone takes to drai -> ans 30
6.6R: Two drones 245 km apart set off at the same moment and fly toward each other along the same line, one at 42 kilometers per hour and the other at 28 ki -> ans 147
6.6R: A hiker leaves a trailhead at a steady 7 kilometers per hour. Four hours later a cyclist leaves the same trailhead on the same path at a steady 21 kil -> ans 42
6.6R: Sprinkler C waters a field in 10 hours and sprinkler D waters the same field in 40 hours, each at its own steady rate. Both run together for 4 hours,  -> ans 20
6.6R: A packing plant running 8 machines for 5 hours a day takes 12 days to finish an order, every machine working at the same steady rate. The next order i -> ans 8
6.6R: A data hall's running cost is proportional to the number of racks and to the hours online, and inversely proportional to its efficiency rating. 20 rac -> ans 1050
6.6R: Two cyclists ride toward each other along a 190 km road. The first sets out from one end at a steady 15 kilometers per hour. Two hours later the secon -> ans 90`
const SPEC = `
WHAT THIS IS. The per-chapter Practice and Challenge problem sets for Quantica Algebra I
chapter 6, "Ratios, Percents, and Proportion". These live in landing.html's PSETS_ALG1 object,
NOT in the lesson files. They are the chapter's capstone, worked after all six lessons.

EXACT SHAPE REQUIRED: 12 practice items and 12 challenge items.
- Practice items carry EXACTLY 1 hint each. Challenge items carry EXACTLY 2 hints each.
- The LAST 3 challenge items, and only those, carry "legendary": true.
- Every "sol" is wrapped in <p>...</p> and closes part 1 on \\(\\boxed{...}\\).

WHAT THE SIX LESSONS TAUGHT, which the sets must span:
- 6.1 Ratios, Simple and Subtle. The common multiplier, so a:b means ak and bk; part-to-part
  against part-to-whole; three-part ratios; a ratio statement cross-multiplies to bx=ay; ratios
  that change when an amount moves; chaining x:y with y:z by matching the shared term; a ratio
  pins proportion but never a count.
- 6.2 Conversion Factors. A conversion factor is a fraction worth 1; units cancel like common
  factors; chains of factors; converting a rate where both units change; squared and cubed units
  needing the factor twice or three times; factors built from a price or a rate of pay.
- 6.3 Percents. p% is the ratio p/100; converting among percent, fraction and decimal; the one
  equation a=(p/100)b with any of the three unknown; finding the whole is a division; the base is
  whatever follows "of"; percents above 100 and below 1.
- 6.4 Percent Problems. The multiplier 1+k/100 for a rise and 1-k/100 for a fall; successive
  changes MULTIPLY and do not add; working backwards is a division by the multiplier; percent
  change is measured against the original; mixture by amount of substance.
- 6.5 Direct and Inverse Proportion. Directly proportional means y/x is constant, inversely
  proportional means xy is constant; telling them apart; proportional to a power; scaling by a
  factor without ever finding k; relationships proportional to neither.
- 6.6 Joint Proportion and Rates. x=kyz and the mixed form x=kyz/w; grouping the varying
  quantities on one side; d=rt; work rate as the fraction of a job done per hour; closing rate as
  a sum when two approach and a difference when one chases.

COVERAGE RULE. Across the 24 items every one of those six lessons must be represented, and the
practice set alone must touch all six. The challenge set goes harder: a ratio that changes twice,
a chain of conversion factors through squared units, successive percent changes run backwards, a
relationship proportional to one quantity and inversely to the square of another, a work-rate
problem with three workers or a partial-shift, and a two-object motion problem where the head
start is stated in time.

FRESHNESS, THE HARD RULE. Below is a catalogue of ALL 175 problems already used in chapter 6's
six lessons. No item may reuse any of those systems, stories, or full number sets. Invent
everything fresh. Every answer in the 24 items must be distinct from every other.

CATALOGUE OF PROBLEMS ALREADY USED (do not reuse any of these):
${USED}
`
const STYLE_PS = `
QUANTICA HOUSE STYLE (follow exactly):
- Voice: problem-first, plainspoken, warm but tight, exactly like AoPS. NO em-dashes. Almost NO
  colons in prose. NO poetic/abstract register, no cute renamings, no dramatized verbs.
- EQUATIONS, LETTERS, NUMBERS AND QUANTITIES DO NOT ACT. Never write that a system "hands over" a
  coefficient, a letter "qualifies", or fractions "ride through" lines. State the fact directly.
- Math in \\( ... \\) inline, $$ ... $$ display. SINGLE backslashes. Bold with <b>...</b>.
- A LITERAL DOLLAR SIGN inside math must be written \\$ so KaTeX does not treat it as a
  delimiter. Prefer writing "dollars" as a word and avoiding the symbol entirely.
- Statement length 120-450 chars. Solutions: easy ~120-320, moderate ~250-450, hard ~350-600.

ITEM SCHEMA:
  { "id", "x", "ans", "accept": [...], "hints": [...], "sol", "legendary"? }
- id is the slot id you were handed, copied VERBATIM.
- Answers are a SINGLE typed value. The grader normAns trims, lowercases, strips spaces and
  commas, and drops a leading plus. It ALSO maps the unicode minus and the en/em dashes onto
  ASCII '-', strips a leading '$', and folds a mixed number like "1 1/2" onto "3/2". So a
  negative answer needs ONLY the ASCII spelling, and a separate unicode-minus entry is dead
  weight the grader can never reach. Never add the whitespace-stripped form of a mixed number
  ("21/2" for "2 1/2"); that now denotes ten and a half and grades a wrong answer correct.
  accept must contain ans plus every reasonable typed variant (word form, exact terminating
  decimal for a fraction, unit spellings).
- EXACTLY ONE ANSWER. A prompt that admits several correct values is broken even when accept
  covers them all, because two students can both be right and type different numbers. Never
  write "give a counterexample", "name a factor", or "find a number such that" when many
  qualify, and never join two questions into one answer box. Pin it with a slick move instead,
  the smallest or largest such value, the sum of all of them over a stated range, their
  product, how many there are, or how many digits the result has. The slick move usually makes
  the problem better, since it forces the student to characterise the whole family rather than
  stop at the first example.
- Practice items: EXACTLY 1 hint. Challenge items: EXACTLY 2 hints. Never a note to yourself.
- sol is wrapped in <p>...</p>, is answer-first, and part 1 closes ON \\(\\boxed{...}\\) with
  nothing trailing after the box in that sentence. An optional second sentence follows the box.
- The last three challenge items (CH10, CH11, CH12) carry "legendary": true. No others do.
`

phase('Blueprint')
const LENSES = [
  'COVERAGE designer. Priority: that all six lessons of the chapter are represented across the 24 items, and that the practice set ramps gently from testing a pair to a three-letter system.',
  'DIFFICULTY designer. Priority: the challenge set, and especially the three legendary items, which must be genuinely hard and reward insight rather than grinding. Each should have a short clever route and a long dull one.',
  'FRESHNESS designer. Priority: that nothing collides with the 176 problems already used in the chapter. Propose distinct systems, distinct stories and distinct answers, and say for each item what makes it different from the catalogue.',
]
const designs = await parallel(LENSES.map((lens, i) => () =>
  agent(`You are designing (NOT writing) the Quantica Algebra I chapter ${CHAPTER} problem sets, "${CHAPTER_TITLE}". ${lens}\n${STYLE_PS}\nSPEC:${SPEC}\nReturn a compact design listing all 24 slots, PR1 to PR12 then CH1 to CH12, each with the exact statement, the exact ask, the exact single-value answer, and which lesson it exercises.`,
    { label: `design:${['coverage', 'difficulty', 'freshness'][i]}`, phase: 'Blueprint' })))

const blueprint = await agent(
  `You are the judge for the Quantica Algebra I chapter ${CHAPTER} problem sets. Merge the three designs into ONE final blueprint covering slots PR1 to PR12 and CH1 to CH12. COMPUTE every answer yourself, digit by digit, stating the full solve, and check each back into the original statement. Every answer distinct from every other; nothing reused from the catalogue; all six lessons represented. CH10, CH11 and CH12 are the legendary three. Return structured plain text keyed by slot id.\n${STYLE_PS}\nSPEC:${SPEC}\n\nA:\n${designs[0]}\n\nB:\n${designs[1]}\n\nC:\n${designs[2]}`,
  { label: 'judge:merge', phase: 'Blueprint' })

phase('Author')
async function tryAgent(prompt, opts, tries = 3) {
  for (let i = 0; i < tries; i++) {
    const r = await agent(prompt, i === 0 ? opts : { ...opts, label: `${opts.label}:r${i}` })
    if (r) return r
    log(`${opts.label} returned null, retry ${i + 1}/${tries - 1}`)
  }
  return null
}
const ITEM_SCHEMA = { type: 'object', properties: { items: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'object', properties: {
  id: { type: 'string' }, x: { type: 'string' }, ans: { type: 'string' },
  accept: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } }, hints: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } },
  sol: { type: 'string' }, legendary: { type: 'boolean' },
}, required: ['id', 'x', 'ans', 'accept', 'hints', 'sol'] } } }, required: ['items'] }

const COMMON = `${STYLE_PS}\nSPEC:${SPEC}\nFINAL BLUEPRINT (authoritative; do NOT change any number, any answer or any statement):\n${blueprint}\n
OUTPUT RULE: return ONLY the slots you were asked for, each carrying its "id" copied VERBATIM.`

const PR = ['PR1','PR2','PR3','PR4','PR5','PR6','PR7','PR8','PR9','PR10','PR11','PR12']
const CH = ['CH1','CH2','CH3','CH4','CH5','CH6','CH7','CH8','CH9','CH10','CH11','CH12']
const chunk = (a, n) => a.reduce((o, v, i) => (i % n ? o[o.length - 1].push(v) : o.push([v]), o), [])
const jobs = []
for (const ids of chunk(PR, 4)) {
  jobs.push(() => tryAgent(`Write PRACTICE slots ${ids.join(', ')} exactly as the blueprint specifies. EXACTLY 1 hint each, no legendary flag, sol wrapped in <p>...</p> and answer-first. Do not change any number or answer.\n${COMMON}`,
    { label: `author:${ids[0]}-${ids[ids.length-1]}`, phase: 'Author', schema: ITEM_SCHEMA }))
}
for (const ids of chunk(CH, 4)) {
  const leg = ids.some(i => ['CH10','CH11','CH12'].includes(i))
  jobs.push(() => tryAgent(`Write CHALLENGE slots ${ids.join(', ')} exactly as the blueprint specifies. EXACTLY 2 hints each, sol wrapped in <p>...</p> and answer-first. ${leg ? 'Set "legendary": true on CH10, CH11 and CH12 only.' : 'Do NOT set legendary on any of these.'} Do not change any number or answer.\n${COMMON}`,
    { label: `author:${ids[0]}-${ids[ids.length-1]}`, phase: 'Author', schema: ITEM_SCHEMA }))
}
const parts = await parallel(jobs)
const bag = {}
for (const p of parts) if (p && p.items) for (const it of p.items) bag[String(it.id).trim().toUpperCase()] = it
const missing = []
function pick(ids, nh, legendarySet) {
  const out = []
  for (const id of ids) {
    const it = bag[id]
    if (!it) { missing.push(id); continue }
    const o = { x: it.x, ans: it.ans, accept: it.accept, hints: it.hints, sol: it.sol }
    if (legendarySet && legendarySet.includes(id)) o.legendary = true
    out.push(o)
  }
  return out
}
const practice = pick(PR, 1, null)
const challenge = pick(CH, 2, ['CH10','CH11','CH12'])
if (missing.length) log(`MISSING SLOTS: ${missing.join(', ')}`)
log(`assembled practice ${practice.length}, challenge ${challenge.length}, legendary ${challenge.filter(c=>c.legendary).length}`)
if (practice.length < 10 || challenge.length < 10) return { error: 'author incomplete', missing, practice, challenge }

phase('Verify')
const VERDICT = { type: 'object', properties: {
  pass: { type: 'boolean' }, issues: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } },
  corrected: { type: 'string' }, pyexpr: { type: 'string' },
}, required: ['pass','issues','corrected','pyexpr'] }
const targets = [...practice.map((b,i)=>({kind:'practice',idx:i,b})), ...challenge.map((b,i)=>({kind:'challenge',idx:i,b}))]
const verdicts = await parallel(targets.map(t => () =>
  tryAgent(`Adversarially verify this single Quantica Algebra I chapter 5 problem-set item. BREAK it before passing.
1. SOLVE IT YOURSELF from scratch. Then read the question again to see WHICH value it asks for. Does it match ans exactly?
2. AMBIGUITY. Could a careful reader read the statement a different way? Is every unit stated? Is the ask unmistakable? Could two students both be RIGHT while typing DIFFERENT numbers? If so the problem is broken and needs a slick move (smallest, largest, sum of all, product, how many, number of digits) to pin exactly one answer.
3. Grader normAns trims, lowercases, strips spaces and commas, drops a leading plus, maps the unicode minus and en/em dashes onto ASCII '-', strips a leading '$', and folds a mixed number like "1 1/2" onto "3/2". Is ans in accept? Any two accept entries that differ ONLY by one of those (dead weight the grader can never reach)? Any accept entry that falsely accepts a wrong response? Any obvious correct form missing?
4. HINT COUNT: a practice item has EXACTLY 1 hint, a challenge item EXACTLY 2. No note-to-self text.
5. sol wrapped in <p>...</p>, answer-first, part 1 closing ON \\(\\boxed{...}\\).
6. KaTeX valid and balanced, SINGLE backslashes, no raw unescaped dollar sign inside math, no em-dashes, colon-free prose, no verb of agency on a mathematical object.
7. In pyexpr give one Python expression computing the answer mechanically. "" only if not a number.
If anything fails: pass=false, list issues, return the full corrected item JSON in "corrected".
ITEM (${t.kind} ${t.idx}):\n${JSON.stringify(t.b, null, 1)}`,
    { label: `verify:${t.kind}${t.idx}`, phase: 'Verify', schema: VERDICT })))
let fixedCount = 0
const pyChecks = []
verdicts.forEach((v,k)=>{
  if (!v) return
  const t = targets[k]
  if (!v.pass && v.corrected) {
    try {
      const nb = JSON.parse(v.corrected)
      if (nb && nb.x && nb.ans) {
        delete nb.id
        if (t.kind==='practice') practice[t.idx]=nb; else { if (t.idx>=9) nb.legendary=true; challenge[t.idx]=nb }
        fixedCount++
      }
    } catch(e) { log(`verify:${t.kind}${t.idx} correction unparseable`) }
  }
  const fb = t.kind==='practice'?practice[t.idx]:challenge[t.idx]
  if (v.pyexpr) pyChecks.push({ where:`${t.kind}[${t.idx}]`, pyexpr:v.pyexpr, ans:fb.ans })
})
log(`verification done, ${fixedCount} corrected, ${pyChecks.length} python checks`)

phase('Audit')
const AUDIT = { type: 'object', properties: {
  findings: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'object', properties: {
    severity: { type: 'string', enum: ['major','minor'] }, where: { type: 'string' }, issue: { type: 'string' }, fix: { type: 'string' },
  }, required: ['severity','where','issue','fix'] } }, verdict: { type: 'string' },
}, required: ['findings','verdict'] }
const AP = `Audit of the Quantica Algebra I chapter 5 problem sets, the last gate before ship. Check:
1. COVERAGE. Which of the six lessons does each item exercise? Report any lesson not represented.
2. FRESHNESS against the catalogue in the spec. Flag any reused system, story or full number set.
3. DISTINCTNESS. Flag any two items in the 24 sharing an answer or a full number set.
4. HINT COUNTS: practice exactly 1, challenge exactly 2. LEGENDARY: exactly the last three challenge items.
5. AMBIGUITY, and any item underdetermined by its own words.
6. RE-SOLVE EVERY ITEM and confirm the value asked for is the value in ans.
7. STYLE: no em-dash, colon-free prose, no verb of agency, single backslashes, no raw dollar sign inside math, sol wrapped in <p> and answer-first with part 1 closing ON the box.
8. DIFFICULTY ORDER: practice ramps gently, challenge is genuinely harder, and the three legendary items are the hardest in the chapter.
For any fix that changes numbers, state the full re-solve so it can be checked before it is applied.
SPEC:${SPEC}\n`
const [aP, aC] = await parallel([
  () => tryAgent(`${AP}\nAudit the PRACTICE set (12 items).\n${JSON.stringify(practice,null,1)}`, { label:'audit:practice', phase:'Audit', schema:AUDIT }),
  () => tryAgent(`${AP}\nAudit the CHALLENGE set (12 items). The practice set follows for collision checking only.\n${JSON.stringify(challenge,null,1)}\n\nPRACTICE FOR COLLISION CHECKING ONLY:\n${JSON.stringify(practice.map(p=>({x:p.x,ans:p.ans})),null,1)}`, { label:'audit:challenge', phase:'Audit', schema:AUDIT }),
])
const audit = {
  findings: [...((aP&&aP.findings)||[]).map(f=>({...f, where:`PRACTICE ${f.where}`})), ...((aC&&aC.findings)||[]).map(f=>({...f, where:`CHALLENGE ${f.where}`}))],
  verdict: `practice: ${(aP&&aP.verdict)||'MISSING'} || challenge: ${(aC&&aC.verdict)||'MISSING'}`,
}
return { practice, challenge, audit, fixedCount, pyChecks, missing }
